%I A399320 #11 Sep 19 2026 20:41:04
%S A399320 32,36,80,112,150,225,242,243,252,338,396,441,468,490,544,608,612,684,
%T A399320 736,828,880,891,928,945,992,1040,1044,1050,1053,1116,1184,1225,1232,
%U A399320 1312,1332,1360,1376,1377,1456,1476,1504,1520,1539,1548,1650,1692,1696,1815,1840
%N A399320 Numbers k such that A008479(k) = 5.
%C A399320 Numbers k such that exactly 4 other numbers m < k share the squarefree kernel A007947(k) = rad(k). See A397742 for further information about classifying numbers in this way.
%C A399320 Terms are in column 5 of A285321.
%C A399320 Superset of A050997 (fifth powers of primes).
%C A399320 Superset of S = {k^2 : k in A138109}.
%C A399320 This sequence, without A050997 and S, is in A332785 (neither squarefree nor powerful).
%C A399320 In the following partition description, p, q, r, s are primes with p < q < r < s when they occur together. Let m = rad(k). Let W be the ordered set of the 4 smaller numbers that share the squarefree kernel, m.
%C A399320 We may partition this sequence into 7 blocks as follows:
%C A399320   A. Numbers k of the form m*p^4 (in A398443),
%C A399320      with W = {m, m*p, m*p^2, m*p^3}.
%C A399320      Includes A050997 (fifth powers of primes).
%C A399320   B. Numbers k of the form m*q (in A398442),
%C A399320      with W = {m, m*p, m*p^2, m*p^3}.
%C A399320   C. Numbers k of the form m*p^3 (in A397459),
%C A399320      with W = {m, m*p, m*p^2, m*q}.
%C A399320   D. Numbers k of the form m*r (in A398864),
%C A399320      with either W = {m, m*p, m*p^2, m*q}
%C A399320      or W = {m, m*p, m*q, m*p^2}.
%C A399320   E. Numbers k of the form m*p*q (in A398444),
%C A399320      with W = {m, m*p, m*q, m*p^2}.
%C A399320   F. Numbers k of the form m*p^2 (in A397458),
%C A399320      with W = {m, m*p, m*q, m*r}.
%C A399320   G. Numbers k of the form m*s (in A398948)
%C A399320      with W = {m, m*p, m*q, m*r}.
%C A399320 Each squarefree number A005117(j) occurs as m = rad(k) for exactly one term k, specifically for k = A284311(5, j-1). So every squarefree number m corresponds to a set W in exactly one of the blocks.
%C A399320 Smallest term k with j >= 3 distinct prime factors is 5*A002110(j).
%H A399320 Michael De Vlieger, <a href="/A399320/b399320.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A399320 Let T(n,k) be the array defined by A284311.
%F A399320 {a(n)} = {T(5,k) : k >= 1}.
%F A399320 {a(n)} = {A065642(A398804(j)) : j >= 1} = {A065642^4(A005117(j)) : j > 1}.
%e A399320 Table of n, a(n) for select n:
%e A399320       n   block       a(n)
%e A399320   ---------------------------------------------------------
%e A399320       1    A           32   2^5
%e A399320       2    E           36   2^2 * 3^2
%e A399320       3    C           80   2^4 * 5
%e A399320       4    C          112   2^4 * 7
%e A399320       5    D          150   2 * 3 * 5^2
%e A399320       7    B          242   2 * 11^2
%e A399320      14    D          490   2 * 5 * 7^2
%e A399320      15    A          544   2^5 * 17
%e A399320      24    F          945   3^3 * 5 * 7
%e A399320      28    D         1050   2 * 3 * 5^2 * 7
%e A399320     297    D        11550   2 * 3 * 5^2 * 7 * 11
%e A399320    1665    G        65065   5 * 7 * 11 * 13^2
%e A399320    3843    D       150150   2 * 3 * 5^2 * 7 * 11 * 13
%e A399320   64968    D      2552550   2 * 3 * 5^2 * 7 * 11 * 13 * 17
%t A399320 rad[x_] := Times @@ FactorInteger[x][[All, 1]]; Select[Range[1840], Function[{k, r}, Count[Range[k], _?(rad[#] == r &)] == 5] @@ {#, rad[#]} &]
%Y A399320 Cf. A005117, A007947, A008479, A065642, A138109, A332785, A397458, A397459, A398442, A398443, A398444, A398864, A398948.
%Y A399320 Row 5 of A284311 (or A285321), sorted. See A398804 for row 4; A397742 for row 3 and further related xrefs.
